{"hash":"fcb734737a8b8798","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":91.943,"said":"Here, at the Ganga Dwar, Kashi opens to the river. Below you the steps of the ghat run down into Maa Ganga. Notice which way she flows. For most of her course she runs east, but here at Kashi she bends and flows north, as if turning back toward the Himalaya she came from, and people say this is part of what makes this bank so holy. How did she come down to earth at all? The Ramayana tells it. A king named Bhagiratha, of the same solar line into which Rama would be born, longed to free the souls of his ancestors, the sixty thousand sons of King Sagara, who had been burned to ashes by the sage Kapila. Only the waters of heaven could purify them. Bhagiratha practised austerities for years, and Ganga agreed to descend; but the force of her fall would have shattered the earth. So Bhagiratha prayed to Shiva. Shiva stood beneath the sky and caught the falling river in his matted hair, and let her out gently, in streams. Bhagiratha led her across the land, and where her water touched the ashes, his ancestors were freed. That is why Shiva wears the Ganga in his locks. Now go down to the water’s edge. Something from the far south is waiting there.\n\nDid you know? Before the Dham, this stretch was reached from the temple only through the lanes; the Ganga Dwar now opens on Lalita Ghat, next to the Nepali temple.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1155},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1171,"end":1320}],"words":[[0.098,0.409,0,4],[0.711,0.156,6,8],[0.879,0.108,9,12],[1.0,0.373,13,18],[1.384,0.445,19,23],[2.057,0.409,25,30],[2.478,0.421,31,36],[2.911,0.132,37,39],[3.055,0.072,40,43],[3.139,0.493,44,49],[4.495,0.373,51,56],[4.88,0.168,57,60],[5.06,0.108,61,64],[5.18,0.397,65,70],[5.589,0.096,71,73],[5.697,0.12,74,77],[5.829,0.349,78,82],[6.19,0.252,83,86],[6.454,0.3,87,91],[6.767,0.252,92,96],[7.031,0.156,97,100],[7.199,0.457,101,106],[8.546,0.457,108,114],[9.014,0.204,115,120],